  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 19dd4d1ffd6eba64688371bde51c8923, type: 3}
  m_Name: Livro 1
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  textString:
  - Read
  - THE SECOND CANON. CONCERNING THE NATURE AND PROPERTIES OF JUPITER.
  - In that which is manifest (that is to say, the body of Jupiter) the other six
    corporeal metals are spiritually concealed, but one more deeply and more tenaciously
    than another. Jupiter has nothing of a Quintessence in his composition, but is
    of the nature of the four elements. On this account this liquefaction is brought
    about by the application of a moderate fire, and, in like manner, he is coagulated
    by moderate cold. He has affinity with the liquefactions of all the other metals.
    For the more like he is to some other nature, the more easily he is united thereto
    by conjunction. For the operation of those nearly allied is easier and more natural
    than of those which are remote. The remote body does not press upon the other.
    At the same time, it is not feared, though it may be very powerful.
